required nuisances: ['sequential_outcome_regressions', 'treatment_and_censoring_mechanisms']
assumptions:
  - consistency: each observed history equals the potential history under its realized regimen
  - no interference: one unit's potential history does not depend on other units' regimens
  - sequential exchangeability in the form the chosen interpretation names: strong sequential randomization (Díaz et al. 2023, Assumption 3) for the modified treatment policy effect, or standard sequential randomization (Assumption 2) for the LMTP-SI of their Theorem 1 (i)
  - positivity: the dose a policy assigns, d(a_t, h_t), lies in the support of A_t given h_t wherever a_t does (Díaz et al. 2023, Assumption 1, which Williams & Díaz 2025 leave unchanged), and so does remaining under observation
  - each policy is known and fixed before the fit, or its cap is a declared tuner with a declared target (X40 part (e)), and on a continuous node it is piecewise smooth invertible (Assumption 4); a policy reads the history and the unit's own treatment at its node, and its earlier treatment columns stand for the intervened values
interpretations, each with the assumption it adds:
  - modified treatment policy effect: the mean outcome had each node's natural dose a_t been replaced by d_t(a_t, h_t), with the history the earlier replacements produce
      needs strong sequential randomization: at every node, every common cause of the dose, the outcome, and the later covariates and doses is in the recorded history (Díaz et al. 2023, Assumption 3; the strong exchangeability of Williams & Díaz 2025, A_t independent of Y(a_t, d_{t+1}(A_{t+1}(a_t), H_{t+1}(a_t))) given H_t)
      source: Díaz, Williams, Hoffman & Schenck (2023), Theorem 1 (ii) and Assumption 3
      source: Williams & Díaz (2025)
  - stochastic intervention (LMTP-SI): the mean outcome had each node's dose been drawn from the law of d_t(A_t, H_t) given the history
      needs standard sequential randomization: at every node, every common cause of the dose and the next covariates, which at the last node are the outcome, is in the recorded history (Díaz et al. 2023, Assumption 2)
      source: Díaz, Williams, Hoffman & Schenck (2023), Theorem 1 (i) and Assumption 2
